  "learningIntroduction": {
    "requirement": "recommended",
    "title": "Seeing Color as a Visual System",
    "summary": "Distinguish color properties, relationships, spatial effects, and meanings before organizing the puzzle.",
    "estimatedMinutes": 4,
    "revision": 1,
    "content": {
      "text": "# What Color Is Doing\n\nColor is often described as decoration, but in visual art it can perform many kinds of work at once. It can distinguish one form from another, organize a composition, suggest depth, create emphasis, establish mood, and carry cultural or symbolic meaning.\n\nA useful first step is to separate several questions that are easy to blur together.\n\n## A color has properties\n\nEvery visible color can be described through properties such as:\n\n- **hue**: the family commonly named as red, blue, green, and so on;\n- **value**: how light or dark the color appears;\n- **saturation**: how intense or muted it appears; and\n- **opacity**: how much it blocks or reveals what lies beneath it.\n\nChanging one property can alter the effect of a color even when the hue remains recognizable.\n\n## Colors act in relation to other colors\n\nA color rarely appears alone. The same patch of gray can seem lighter against black and darker against white. A red may appear more intense beside green than beside orange. A restricted set of colors can make small differences especially noticeable.\n\nThis means that color perception is relational. What a color appears to do depends partly on the colors around it.\n\n## Color can organize space\n\nArtists can use color to separate a subject from its surroundings, draw attention to one area, or suggest that some forms are nearer than others.\n\nDistant forms are often represented with reduced contrast and less saturated color. Strong differences may pull an area forward. Similar colors may visually join forms that are physically separate.\n\nThese are conventions and perceptual tendencies, not mechanical laws.\n\n## Color can imitate or depart from appearance\n\nSome artworks use color naturalistically, trying to resemble the colors of observed things under particular light. Others alter color deliberately.\n\nA face may be painted green, a landscape purple, or a shadow bright orange. Such choices can express emotion, create rhythm, establish symbolism, or call attention to the act of representation itself.\n\nDeparture from natural appearance is not necessarily an error. It may be the point.\n\n## Meaning is not universal\n\nColors often carry associations, but those associations vary across time, place, community, and situation. White can suggest purity in one context and mourning in another. Red can signify danger, celebration, political identity, sacred meaning, or simply a visual accent.\n\nInterpretation therefore requires evidence from the artwork and its context rather than a fixed dictionary of color meanings.\n\n---\n\nAs you begin the puzzle, consider an image in which color strongly affected your response. Was the effect produced by one color alone, or by its relationship to other colors and forms?\n",
      "mediaType": "text/markdown"
    }
  },
